      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ γὰρ τὸ φονευθῆναι κακόν ἐστι, κἂν μὴ πάντες λέγωσιν ὅτι μηδεὶς αὐτοπαθεῖν θέλει",
          "english_quote": "For being murdered is evil, even if not everyone says that no one wants to experience it himself",
          "issue": "The draft takes ὅτι as introducing the content of λέγωσιν. This is grammatically defensible, but a material contextual alternative takes ὅτι causally: being murdered is evil, even if not everyone says so, because nobody wants to undergo it himself. The alternatives change whether unwillingness to suffer murder is Peter’s asserted justification or the content of a statement not universally made. The draft does not disclose this attachment decision.",
          "suggested_revision": "If retaining the main text, add a source-quoted note identifying the content-clause attachment, explaining its basis in λέγωσιν ὅτι, and preserving the causal alternative. Another candidate for fresh review is: “For being murdered is evil, even if not everyone says so, because no one wants to undergo it himself,” accompanied by the current reading as an alternative. This is a disclosure revision, not an unresolved-source block.",
